<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanggyeong</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sanggyeong Yongcheonbu, Shangjing Longquanfu, Upper Capital of Balhae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanggyeong, the Upper Capital, was the main capital of the kingdom of Balhae, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudan-river">Mudan River</a> valley near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ning-an">Ning'an</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. King Mun moved the court there around 756, and apart from a short interval it remained the capital until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> conquered Balhae in 926. Laid out on the grid plan of the Tang capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, its walls, palace foundations and temple sites survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanggyeong.jpg" alt="Sanggyeong" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 호고호 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B8%A4%E6%B5%B7%E5%9B%BD%E4%B8%8A%E4%BA%AC%E9%BE%99%E6%B3%89%E5%BA%9C%E5%AE%AB%E5%9F%8E%E9%81%97%E5%9D%80_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
